# Approval gate (Harry decides)

You must **not** implement big changes until the user explicitly approves.

## Magic words that mean GO
Treat any of these as approval to implement the **last plan you proposed** (or the plan in the same message if they pasted one):
- `go`
- `GO`
- `approved`
- `lfg`
- `ship it`
- `do it`
- `implement`

If they say `go` **and** change the plan in the same message, follow the updated plan.

## Magic words that mean STOP / revise
- `stop`, `wait`, `no`, `change plan`, `revise`
→ Do not write more code. Ask what to change. Propose a new short plan.

## When the user proposes an idea or a feature

**Phase A — Plan only (default).** Do **not** edit files yet.

Reply with a short plan in this shape:

```text
## Plan (waiting for GO)
Goal: …
Demo path: 1) … 2) … 3) …
Files I will touch: …
APIs / tables: …
Stack pieces: … (nothing extra)
Out of scope: …
Risks: …
## After I finish I will tell you to: …
Reply **go** to implement, or tell me what to change.
```

Keep the plan short (roughly half a screen). No essays.

**Phase B — Implement.** Only after GO.
- Follow the approved plan. Smallest diff.
- Do not expand scope mid-flight.
- If you discover a blocker, stop and re-ask (new mini-plan).

**Phase C — Next steps.** After implementing, always end with:

```text
## Done
- What changed: …
- How to verify: …
## Your next steps
1. …
2. …
3. …
## Waiting
Say **go** if you want the next chunk (name it), or describe the next idea.
```

## What counts as “needs approval”
- New pages, new API routes, new dependencies
- Scaffold / boilerplate / Product MVP builds
- Auth, AI, database wiring, refactors across many files
- Deleting files or changing env / deploy setup

## What does NOT need a full plan first (still keep diffs small)
- Answering questions (“what is Zod?”)
- Explaining errors without editing
- Tiny one-line fixes the user already named exactly (“rename title to X”)
- Pure doc clarifications the user asked to write into a markdown file they named

If unsure whether something is big: **plan first**.

## Prompts that include “just build it” / full scaffold text
If the user pastes a long scaffold prompt from `Prompt.md` that already says to build, still give a **5-bullet plan** first and ask for `go`, unless their message also contains a GO word.

Exception: user says `go` in the **same** message as the scaffold prompt → implement.
